{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Scj9VukmMPq3jguZVyNDrK","type":"tool_result","content":"> `(4/ε) · ln(1/ε) · (1 + 1/ε²)`. Also it is easy to check that the bound in\n> Theorem 2 valid not only for 3-regular graphs but for graphs of maximum\n> vertex degree at most three as well.\n>\n> **Theorem 3** (Ellis et al. [6]). For any tree `T` on `n ≥ 3` vertices,\n> `pw(T) ≤ log_3 n`.\n>\n> **Lemma 4.** Let `G` be a graph on `n` vertices and with maximum vertex\n> degree at most 3. Then for any vertex subset `X ⊆ V(G)` there is a path\n> decomposition `P = (X_1, X_2, …, X_r)` of `G` of width at most\n> `max{|X|, n/3 + 1} + (2/3) log_3 n + 1` and such that `X = X_r`.\n>\n> **Theorem 5.** For any `ε > 0`, there exists an integer `n_ε` such that\n> for every graph `G` with maximum vertex degree at most three and with\n> `|V(G)| > n_ε`, `pw(G) ≤ (1/6 + ε)|V(G)|`.\n>\n> *Proof.* For `ε > 0`, let `G` be a graph on `n > n_ε = (8/ε) · ln(1/ε) · (1 + 1/ε²)`\n> vertices and with maximum vertex degree at most three. By Theorem 2, there\n> is a bisection `V_1, V_2` of `G` such that there is at most `(1/6 + ε/2)|V(G)|`\n> edges with endpoints in `V_1` and `V_2`. Let `∂(V_1)` (`∂(V_2)`) be the set\n> of vertices in `V_1` (`V_2`) having a neighbor in `V_2` (`V_1`). Note that\n> `|∂(V_i)| ≤ (1/6 + ε/2) n`, `i = 1, 2`. By Lemma 4, there is a path\n> decomposition `P_1 = (A_1, …, A_p)` of `G[V_1]` and a path decomposition\n> `P_3 = (C_1, …, C_s)` of `G[V_2]` of width at most\n> `max{(1/6 + ε/2) n, n/6 + 1} + (2/3) log_3 n + 1 ≤ (1/6 + ε) n` such that\n> `A_p = ∂(V_1)` and `C_1 = ∂(V_2)`. It remains to show how the path\n> decomposition `P` of `G` can be obtained from path decompositions `P_1` and\n> `P_3`. To construct `P` we show that there is a path decomposition\n> `P_2 = (B_1, …, B_r)` of `G[∂(V_1) ∪ ∂(V_2)]` of width `≤ (1/6 + ε) n` and\n> with `B_1 = ∂(V_1)`, `B_r = ∂(V_2)`. The union of `P_1`, `P_2`, and `P_3`,\n> i.e., `(A_1, …, A_p, B_1, …, B_r, C_1, …, C_s)` will be the path\n> decomposition of `G` of width `≤ (1/6 + ε) n`. The path decomposition `P_2`\n> is constructed as follows. We put `B_1 = ∂(V_1)`. In a bag `B_j`, where\n> `j ≥ 1` is odd, we choose a vertex `v ∈ B_j ∖ ∂(V_2)`. We put\n> `B_{j+1} = B_j ∪ (N(v) ∩ ∂(V_2))` and `B_{j+2} = B_{j+1} ∖ {v}`. […] we argue\n> that for any `j`, `|B_j| ≤ (1/6 + ε) n + 1`. [the count by `D_m`, the\n> vertices of `∂(V_1)` with exactly `m` neighbours in `∂(V_2)`:\n> `|D_1| + 2|D_2| + 3|D_3| ≤ (1/6 + ε) n`, so `|B_1| ≤ (1/6 + ε) n − |D_2| − 2|D_3|`\n> and every bag is within `|D_2| + 2|D_3| + 1` of `|B_1|`.]\n\nSo the base case is a **one-level** construction: one near-equal bisection\nwith few cut edges (Monien–Preis), Lemma 4 on each side with the boundary as\nthe end bag (its `n/3 + 1` and `(2/3) log_3 n` come from the tree pathwidth\nbound, Theorem 3), and a sweep across the boundary. Nothing recursive.\n\n### 0.3 The two hypotheses the formalisation is conditional on\n\nThe chain `Lemma 5.1 → Corollary 5.2` uses three external facts and nothing\nelse beyond Theorem 3.2 (which is self-contained combinatorics) and\n`pw(G) ≤ |X| + pw(G − X)` (which is `pathwidth_le_pathwidth_induce_add_card_compl`\nin `TreeBound.lean`):\n=======\n### 4.3 What Fomin–Høie's one-level argument adds (stated, not proved)\n\nIterating C2s with Monien–Preis cuts of `(1/6 + ε)` of each piece sums to\n`n/3`, not `n/6` (plan §0.4 (3)). Fomin & Høie (2006, Theorem 5) reach\n`(1/6 + ε) n` for maximum degree 3 with **one** level and two ingredients\nthat are not in `CutLemma.lean`, quoted from the plan's §0.2 (their IPL\npaper is held):\n\n> **Lemma 4** (Fomin & Høie 2006). Let `G` be a graph on `n` vertices and with\n> maximum vertex degree at most 3. Then for any vertex subset `X ⊆ V(G)` there\n> is a path decomposition `P = (X₁, X₂, …, X_r)` of `G` of width at most\n> `max{|X|, n/3 + 1} + (2/3) log₃ n + 1` and such that `X = X_r`.\n\nWith a bisection `V₁, V₂` of at most `(1/6 + ε/2) n` cut edges (Monien &\nPreis, their Theorem 2) they take Lemma 4 on `G[V₁]` with `X = ∂V₁` as the\n*last* bag and on `G[V₂]` with `X = ∂V₂` as the *first* bag — each of width\nat most `max{(1/6 + ε/2) n, n/6 + 1} + (2/3) log₃ n + 1 ≤ (1/6 + ε) n` — and\njoin them through a **sweep** `P₂ = (B₁, …, B_r)` of `G[∂V₁ ∪ ∂V₂]` with\n`B₁ = ∂V₁`, `B_r = ∂V₂`: at an odd `j` pick `v ∈ B_j ∖ ∂V₂`, put\n`B_{j+1} = B_j ∪ (N(v) ∩ ∂V₂)` and `B_{j+2} = B_{j+1} ∖ {v}`; counting the\nvertices of `∂V₁` by their number `m` of neighbours in `∂V₂`\n(`|D₁| + 2|D₂| + 3|D₃| ≤ (1/6 + ε) n`), every bag stays within\n`|D₂| + 2|D₃| + 1` of `|B₁|`, so `|B_j| ≤ (1/6 + ε) n + 1`. In the vocabulary\nof this file, the sweep is a path decomposition of `G[∂V₁ ∪ ∂V₂]` whose bags\nare at most `|∂V₁| + 1`, i.e. the middle bag `∂V₁ ∪ ∂V₂` of C1's third term\nis replaced by a sequence of bags each of size about `|∂V₁|`, and Lemma 4\nmakes the boundary the end bag of each side so that the three pieces\nconcatenate. As a lemma in the file's terms:\n\n> **Lemma (Fomin–Høie transfer; stated, not proved).** For `G` of maximum\n> degree 3 and a partition `V = V₁ ⊔ V₂` with `c` cut edges,\n> `pw(G) ≤ max{c, n/6 + 1} + (2/3) log₃ n + 2`.\n\nIts proof needs Lemma 4 (a decomposition of a degree-3 graph with a\nprescribed end bag, whose `n/3 + 1` and `(2/3) log₃ n` come from Theorem 3,\n`pw(T) ≤ log₃ n` for trees — `tree_pathwidth_le_log` in `TreeBound.lean` is\nthat tree case) and the sweep. Neither is attempted here; both are a\ncandidate for item 11. C1 and C2 are what a general graph gives with no\ndegree bound; they are not how Fomin–Høie reach `n/6`.\n\n### 4.4 Kill verdict, and what this does not claim\n\n**Kill verdict: not met.** No counterexample in the check (every graph to 8\nvertices for the one-cut statements, 10,000 random trees for the iterated\nones); C2 fitted in Lean in the list-indexed form, so the fallback \"C1 alone,\nC2 stated in the report\" was not needed.\n\n**Does not claim.** Fomin–Høie's `